ench/.claude/skills/dependency-auditor | head -50"], ["Read", "/private/tmp/wk/ddc7c7186a/workbench/.claude/skills/dependency-auditor/SKILL.md"]], "final_message": "Yes, one blocker: **somelib 1.1 is GPL-3.0**.\n\n| Dependency | License | Closed-source status |\n|---|---|---|\n| requests 2.32 | Apache-2.0 | Fine. Ship the license text and any NOTICE file, and keep the attribution. |\n| somelib 1.1 | GPL-3.0 | **Problem.** See below. |\n| left-pad 0.2 | MIT | Fine. Keep the copyright and license notice in your attributions. |\n\n**Why somelib is a problem**\n- GPL-3.0 is strong copyleft. If you distribute a binary or package that links to or bundles somelib, the combined work generally has to be released under GPL-3.0 with source available. That conflicts with a closed-source product.\n- If the product is a hosted service that users never receive a copy of, the GPL's distribution trigger usually isn't met. This only holds if you never ship it to customers, including as an on-prem install, an installer, a container image, or a desktop or mobile app. If you're unsure, ask counsel. (AGPL would change this, but this one is plain GPL.)\n- GPL-3.0 also has anti-tivoization and patent-license terms, which can matter for hardware or locked-down devices.\n\n**Options, roughly in order of preference**\n1. Replace somelib with a permissively licensed alternative (MIT, BSD or Apache).\n2. Ask the authors for a commercial or dual license, if they offer one.\n3. Isolate it as a separate program that you call over a process or network boundary, not linked into your code. This is a legal gray area, so get a lawyer's sign-off first.\n4. Remove the feature that needs it.\n\n**Limits of this check**\n- `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. Transitive dependencies, for example what requests pulls in, can carry their own licenses and need scanning too.\n- The license labels come from the file. I haven't verified them against the packages. Some projects relicense between versions or carry exceptions, such as the LGPL linking exception.\n- The `dependency-auditor` skill in this repo has no scripts, only a placeholder, so I did no automated scanning. I also didn't check for CVEs. Before release, run a tool like `pip-audit` or `pip-licenses` on the resolved environment.\n- This isn't legal advice. Have counsel review the GPL question before you ship.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}